"A property graph schema diagram defines node labels, relationship types, properties, and applicable graph constraints.\n\nUse when: Design graph traversals around domain relationships. Review graph labels and constraints before loading data.\n\nAvoid when: Readers need individual triples or relational join tables.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes stand for label sets or clearly identified example entities. Directed edges carry specific relationship types. Property compartments distinguish node properties from relationship properties.\n\nBuild: Choose a schema view or an instance example and label it. Identify domain nouns as node concepts and verbs as relationships. Attach properties to the concept or relationship they describe. Lay out common traversals left to right and place edge properties in separate callouts.\n\nCheck: Relationship direction and type have consistent domain meaning. Property ownership is unambiguous. Uniqueness and required-property constraints are shown only if declared. Edge labels and property callouts have clear nonintersecting routes.\n\nPitfalls: Mixing schema labels and concrete entities without a visual distinction. Replacing meaningful relationship verbs with a generic related_to edge.\n\nExample: Person nodes WORK_FOR Organization nodes, with employment start date stored on the relationship.\n\nAlternatives: RDF graph, Ontology diagram."
